# Background

The current generation of RetroForth did not start off as a
traditional, interactive Forth. A minimal (and lightly expanded)
REPL simply called the listener has existed, but it is not a
user friendly system.

Takawiri is a new listener. I've been slowly working on it,
with a goal of growing it into something useful. At present it
is functional, but many pieces remain. (See the end of this
document for a partial list).

# The Interface

Takawiri presents a fullscreen interface. This has a large area
for the display (the "text output buffer", or TOB) with a set
of introspection views on the right. At the bottom is the text
input area.

  +-----------------------------------------+----------------+
  | Text Output Buffer                      | Introspection  |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         +----------------+
  |                                         | Stack Display  |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         +----------------+
  |                                         | Float Display  |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         +----------------+
  |                                         | String Preview |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         +----------------+
  |                                         | Watched Vars   |
  |                                         |                |
  |                                         |                |
  +-----------------------------------------+----------------+
  | input area                                               |
  +----------------------------------------------------------+

# Startup & Dependencies

Takawiri requires RetroForth 2024.9 or newer, with the ioctl
device enabled. It also requires a terminal with 100 or more
columns and at least 32 rows.

I do the initial configuration, and check these, reporting any
errors and exiting if necessary.

~~~
@Version #202601 lt?
  [ 'Requires_RetroForth_2026.1_or_newer s:put nl bye ] if
~~~

Setup the local terminal dimensions. This uses the `ioctl:`
words added in RetroForth 2024.9.

~~~
ioctl:term-size (rows,cols) 'LT:W const 'LT:H const

LT:W #100 lt? [ 'Terminal_too_narrow! s:put nl bye ] if
LT:H #32 lt?  [ 'Terminal_too_short!  s:put nl bye ] if
~~~

Setup the text output buffer dimensions. The width is currently
fixed at 80 columns; the height is set based on the terminal
height.

~~~
#80       'TOB:W const (:-n)
LT:H #2 - 'TOB:H const (:-n)
~~~

Load dependencies from the library.

~~~
{ 'konilo 'termina 'tob } &library:load a:for-each
~~~

# Configure UI Colors

~~~
:dss:label  (:-) fg:red  ;
:dss:value  (:-) fg:yellow ;
:dss:sep    (:-) fg:cyan ;
:dss:prompt (:-) bg:blue fg:white ;
~~~

# Utilities

I intend for takawiri to provide a variety of useful tools to
aid in using RetroForth interactively.

~~~
:,stack (:s-) d:lookup d:stack fetch s:put nl ;
~~~

# UI

First are words to display the text output buffer.

~~~
:bar:right   (:-) dss:sep TOB:H n:inc [ I n:inc TOB:W #2 n:add vt:row,col $| c:put ] indexed-times vt:reset ;
:bar:bottom  (:-) dss:sep TOB:H n:inc #1 vt:row,col TOB:W n:inc [ $= c:put ] times $+ c:put vt:reset ;
:display:tob (:-) tob:refresh tob:display bar:right bar:bottom ;
~~~

Draw the section separators.

~~~
:length (:-n) LT:W TOB:W #4 n:add n:sub ;
:---    (:n-) [ $- c:put ] times sp ;
:sections (:-)
  dss:sep
  #4 [ I n:inc #6 n:mul n:inc TOB:W #4 n:add vt:row,col length --- ]
  indexed-times
  vt:reset ;
~~~

~~~
'Items d:create #0 comma #32 allot

:tos? (:nm-nmf) over n:zero? ;
:dss (:-)
  [ depth #5 n:min !Items
  &Items fetch-next &store-next times drop
  &Items a:reverse [ ] a:for-each
  #0 &Items [
  over n:inc #7 n:add #84 vt:row,col
  dss:label
  tos? [ 'TOS:___ s:put ] [ '_______ s:put ] choose
  vt:reset
  n:put
  n:inc ] a:for-each
  drop ] gc ;
~~~

## String Preview

This is the start of code to display temporary strings on the
data stack. The plan is to have it show below the stack values,
in a format like:

  <depth> <first XX characters>

The <depth> data will match up to the values in the stack disp.

Note: this won't be useful until after the alternate `s:evaluate`
is done.

~~~
  :string? (v-vf) dup STRINGS gt? ;

  :string:display
    over n:inc #19 n:add #84 vt:row,col
    fg:red
    tos? [ 'TOS:___ s:put ] [ '_______ s:put ] choose
    vt:reset
    s:put ;

  :string:not-string
    over n:inc #19 n:add #84 vt:row,col
    fg:blue '_______n/a s:put drop vt:reset ;
  :strings (:-)
    #0 &Items [ string? &string:display &string:not-string choose n:inc  ]
    a:for-each drop ;
~~~

~~~
:layout:stat,col (:-n) #84 ;
:layout:stat     (:sn-) layout:stat,col vt:row,col dss:label s:put dss:value ;

:stats (:-)
  'HERE:__ #1 layout:stat here n:put
  'FREE:__ #2 layout:stat FREE n:put
  'DATA:__ #3 layout:stat depth n:put
  'FLOAT:_ #4 layout:stat f:depth n:put
  'ROW:___ #5 layout:stat @TY n:put
  'COL:___ #6 layout:stat @TX n:put
  vt:reset
;

:fss:clear (:-)
  #5 [ #14 I n:add #84 vt:row,col LT:W #83 n:sub [ sp ] times ] indexed-times ;

:fss:label (n-)
  n:zero? [ 'FTOS:__ s:put ] [ '_______ s:put ] choose ;

:fss (:-)
  fss:clear
  f:depth #5 n:min dup
  [ #14 I n:add #84 vt:row,col
    dss:label I fss:label
    dss:value f:dup f:to-string s:put f:push vt:reset
  ] indexed-times
  &f:pop times ;

:prompt (:-)
  dss:prompt
  LT:H #1 vt:row,col LT:W [ sp ] times
  LT:H #1 vt:row,col '>>_ s:put ;
~~~

# Input

Takawiri reads input in cbreak mode, so it echoes the token itself. This
keeps the input line under its control and makes backspace and delete
visibly erase the preceding character.

~~~
:input:done? (c-f)
  [ ASCII:CR eq? ] [ ASCII:LF eq? ] [ ASCII:SPACE eq? ] tri or or ;

:input:remove-character (:-)
  buffer:size #2 gteq? [ buffer:get drop ] if buffer:get drop ;

:input:backspace? (c-f)
  [ ASCII:BS eq? ] [ ASCII:DEL eq? ] bi or ;

:input:erase (:-)
  buffer:size n:positive? [ #1 vt:left sp #1 vt:left ] if ;

:input:echo (c-)
  dup input:backspace? [ drop input:erase ]
  [ dup input:done? [ drop ] [ c:put ] choose ] choose ;

:input:check-bs (c-c)
  dup input:backspace? [ input:remove-character ] if ;

:input:get-word (-s)
  [ #7 fetch buffer:set
    [ c:get dup buffer:add dup input:echo input:check-bs input:done? ] until
    buffer:start s:chop ] buffer:preserve ;
~~~

# Errors

The standard `err:notfound` hook prints into the current output stream.
Takawiri records the failed token instead. The popup temporarily bypasses
the text output buffer, then marks it for redraw after the dialog closes.

~~~
#0 'NotFound var-n
'NotFoundToken d:create #128 allot

:err:notfound:record (:-)
  TIB &NotFoundToken s:copy
  #-1 !NotFound ;

'PopupRow var
'PopupCol var

:popup:setup (:-)
  #12 !PopupRow #27 !PopupCol ;

:popup:bar (:-) $+ c:put #46 [ $- c:put ] times $+ c:put ;

:popup:line (n-)
  @PopupRow n:add @PopupCol vt:row,col
  $| c:put #46 [ sp ] times $| c:put ;

:notfound:popup (:-)
  popup:setup
  dss:sep @PopupRow @PopupCol vt:row,col popup:bar
  #1 popup:line #2 popup:line
  @PopupRow #3 n:add @PopupCol vt:row,col popup:bar
  dss:label @PopupRow #1 n:add @PopupCol #2 n:add vt:row,col
  'WORD_NOT_FOUND s:put
  dss:value @PopupRow #2 n:add @PopupCol #2 n:add vt:row,col
  'TOKEN:__ s:put &NotFoundToken #36 s:left s:put
  dss:sep @PopupRow #1 n:add @PopupCol #25 n:add vt:row,col
  'Press_any_key s:put
  vt:reset c:get drop #0 !NotFound ;

:dialog:with (q-)
  &c:put unhook call
  &tob:put &c:put set-hook
  tob:refresh ;
~~~

~~~
:quit (:-) ioctl:set-lbreak vt:reset bye ;
:bye  (:-) quit ;
~~~

# Watchlist

The watchlist will allow monitoring a small number of addresses
in the right panel of the interface. A use case might be to
do something like:

    &Base 'Base watch
    &Compiler 'Compiler watch

~~~
'Watchlist       d:create #5 , #-1 , #-1 , #-1 , #-1 , #-1 ,
'WatchlistLabels d:create #5 , #-1 , #-1 , #-1 , #-1 , #-1 ,

:watchlist:find (:a-n)
  dup &Watchlist a:contains? [ drop #-1 ] -if;
  &Watchlist swap a:index ;

:watchlist:make-label (:s-s)
  dup s:length #8 gt? [ #8 s:left ] if
  dup s:length #8 lt?
    [ dup s:length #8 swap n:sub [ '_ s:append ] times ] if
  s:keep ;

:watch (:as-)
  watchlist:make-label
  #-1 watchlist:find &Watchlist &WatchlistLabels
  'abcde 'adcbec reorder a:store a:store ;

:unwatch (:a-)
  watchlist:find dup n:positive? &drop -if
  [ &Watchlist       #-1 'abc 'acab reorder a:store ]
  [ &WatchlistLabels #-1 'abc 'acab reorder a:store ] bi ;

:watchlist (:-)
  #26 #5 [ dup #84 vt:row,col
        dss:label &WatchlistLabels over #26 n:sub a:fetch
          dup #-1 -eq? [ s:put sp ] [ drop '_________ s:put ] choose
        dss:value &Watchlist over #26 n:sub a:fetch
        dup n:positive? [ fetch ] [ drop #0 ] choose
        n:put n:inc vt:reset ] times drop ;
~~~

~~~
:ui (:-)
  &err:notfound:record &err:notfound set-hook
  ioctl:set-cbreak
  &banner tob:with
  [ vt:reset vt:clear vt:home
    display:tob
    sections
    stats dss strings fss watchlist
    prompt input:get-word vt:reset
    [ dup s:put sp interpret
      @NotFound [ &notfound:popup dialog:with ] if ] tob:with
 ] forever ;

ui
~~~

